```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("precompute-setup.R")
unlink(file.path(docs_root, c("my-study", "sci-study")), recursive = TRUE)
```

Every rendehR function works inside a project: a folder with a configuration
file, credentials, and room for the raw and cohort files. This article shows
what a project contains, which settings matter, and how several studies can
share one copy of the raw data.

## The project folder

`ehr_init_project()` creates the folder and its two configuration files. Nothing
is overwritten if they already exist.

```{r init}
library(rendehR)
ehr_init_project(src_name = "mimic_iv_demo", base_dir = "my-study")
```

```{r tree}
list.files("my-study", recursive = TRUE, include.dirs = TRUE)
```

| Path | Holds |
|:--|:--|
| `config/ehr_config.json` | Project settings: database, source, paths, cohort profile |
| `config/config_pass.json` | PostgreSQL and PhysioNet credentials |
| `data/source_data/<source>/` | Raw files from PhysioNet (unless `raw_data_root` points elsewhere) |
| `data/cohorts_data/<source>_parquet/` | The cohort, imported to Parquet |
| `data/duckdb/` | DuckDB files, when you use the DuckDB engine |
| `sql/<source>/concepts_sqls/user/` | Your own SQL concepts (see `vignette("concepts")`) |
| `sql/generated/<source>/` | SQL that rendehR generates from the concept dictionary |

Keep `config/config_pass.json` and `data/` out of version control: one holds a
password, the other patient data.

## The configuration file

```{r config, comment = ""}
cat(readLines("my-study/config/ehr_config.json"), sep = "\n")
```

The settings you are most likely to change:

- **`database.db_name`**: the PostgreSQL database for this project. It defaults to
  `"rendehr"`; give every study its own database. Functions that take `db_name`
  use this value when you leave it out.
- **`paths.raw_data_root`**: where the raw files are. `null` means
  `data/source_data` inside the project. Point it at a shared folder to reuse one
  download in several projects (below).
- **`cohort.profile`**: `"generic"` (the default) or `"scid"`, the spinal cord
  injury reference implementation. The profile decides two defaults. For an SCI cohort selected
  by diagnosis, `"scid"` adds the injury classification to the cohort table;
  any other cohort is left alone. And under `"scid"` the pipeline computes the
  comorbidity indices and injury severity scores unless told not to, while
  under `"generic"` they run only when asked.
- **`cohort.icd_filter_path`**: the ICD list used when a call names no cohort.

Relative paths are relative to `project.base_dir`, which `ehr_init_project()`
writes as an absolute path.

## Credentials

`config_pass.json` holds the PostgreSQL login, and a PhysioNet login for the
credentialed datasets. The PostgreSQL login is for a server you run yourself;
rendehR does not install one (see the README's
[Requirements](https://ate-driven-lab.github.io/rendehR/#requirements)):

```json
{
  "postgres": {"host": "localhost", "port": 5432, "user": "postgres", "password": "..."},
  "physionet": {"username": "...", "password": "..."}
}
```

Environment variables take precedence over the file: `PGHOST`, `PGPORT`, `PGUSER`,
`PGPASSWORD` and `PGSSLMODE` for PostgreSQL, `PHYSIONET_USER` and
`PHYSIONET_PASS` for PhysioNet. The demo datasets are open and need no
PhysioNet login.

## Using a project

`ehr_use_config()` makes a project active for the R session, and
`ehr_check_config()` reports what the settings resolve to:

```{r use}
ehr_use_config("my-study/config/ehr_config.json")
check <- ehr_check_config(quiet = TRUE)
check$ok
check$paths$raw_dir
```

Without an active project, rendehR looks for `config/ehr_config.json` in the
working directory, so `setwd()` into the project works too. You can also pass
`base_dir` and `db_name` to each call.

## Raw data

`download_source()` fetches a PhysioNet release into `raw_data_root`. It checks
each file against the release's SHA-256 manifest, reuses files that already
match, and resumes interrupted downloads.

```r
download_source("mimic_iv_demo")       # open
download_source("mimic_iv")            # needs a credentialed PhysioNet account
```

The supported releases and their size on disk as downloaded:

| Source | Release | Size |
|:--|:--|--:|
| MIMIC-IV demo | 2.2 | 16 MB |
| MIMIC-III demo | 1.4 | 94 MB |
| eICU-CRD demo | 2.0.1 | 53 MB |
| MIMIC-IV | 3.1 | 10 GB |
| MIMIC-III | 1.4 | 6.2 GB |
| eICU-CRD | 2.0 | 5.2 GB |

The cohort import can cache the raw CSV as Parquet next to the project
(`data/raw_parquet_cache/`). That takes about as much space again, and makes
repeated imports of a full source much faster.

## Several studies, one download

A study is a project: its own cohort, database and settings. Point
`raw_data_root` at the folder that already holds the files and nothing is
downloaded twice. Here a second project reuses the demo downloaded in
`vignette("rendehR")`:

```{r share}
ehr_init_project(
  src_name = "mimic_iv_demo", base_dir = "sci-study",
  raw_data_root = normalizePath("rendehr-demo/data/source_data")
)
ehr_check_config(config_path = "sci-study/config/ehr_config.json", quiet = TRUE)$paths$raw_dir
```

Set a different `db_name` in each project's configuration and the two studies
never touch each other's tables.
